course: space
lesson: 9
title: Asteroids and the asteroid belt
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Asteroids are the leftover building blocks from when the planets formed, lumps of rock and metal from pebble size up to hundreds of kilometres across.
explain: Most of them circle the Sun in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter, but it is not like the crowded rock fields of films: the belt is so empty that spacecraft fly through without dodging anything.
explain: All the belt's asteroids together weigh only about 3 percent of our Moon; Jupiter's strong pull stopped them ever joining into a planet.
explain: A few asteroids cross the Earth's path, so astronomers track them, and in 2022 NASA's DART spacecraft even knocked a small one onto a new path as a test of defending the Earth.
explain: 66 million years ago a 10-kilometre asteroid hit what is now Mexico and helped wipe out the dinosaurs, except the birds, whose relatives still sing outside your window.
fact: Asteroids are rocky or metallic leftovers from the birth of the Solar System, ranging from pebbles to hundreds of kilometres across.
fact: Most known asteroids orbit in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter, and more than a million have been catalogued.
fact: The belt is mostly empty space, with asteroids usually hundreds of thousands of kilometres apart.
fact: All the asteroids in the belt put together have only about 3 percent of the mass of our Moon.
fact: Ceres is the largest object in the asteroid belt, about 940 kilometres across, big enough for its gravity to pull it round, so it is classed as a dwarf planet.
fact: Vesta is the second most massive asteroid, and a giant impact on it blasted off rock that still falls to Earth as meteorites.
fact: Some asteroids are dark and rich in carbon, some are stony, and a few are mostly metal, like the asteroid Psyche.
fact: Japanese probes brought back dust from the asteroids Itokawa and Ryugu, and NASA's OSIRIS-REx returned samples from Bennu in 2023.
fact: Some asteroids cross the Earth's orbit, and astronomers track tens of thousands of these near-Earth objects to check whether any could hit us.
fact: In 2022 NASA's DART (Double Asteroid Redirection Test) spacecraft crashed into the small asteroid Dimorphos and changed its orbit, the first test of defending the Earth.
fact: About 66 million years ago an asteroid about 10 kilometres wide hit what is now Mexico and helped wipe out the dinosaurs, except birds.
fact: A meteoroid is a small space rock, a meteor is its streak of light in the sky as it burns up in our air, and a meteorite is a piece that reaches the ground.
exercise: What are asteroids? | Rocky or metallic leftovers from the birth of the Solar System | Pieces of a planet that exploded last century | Balls of ice with long tails | Small stars that have gone out
exercise: Where is the asteroid belt? | Between Mars and Jupiter | Between the Earth and the Moon | Beyond Neptune | Between Mercury and Venus
exercise: How many asteroids have been catalogued? | More than a million | About a hundred or so | Exactly nine, like the planets | About ten thousand of them
exercise: How much mass do all the belt asteroids have together? | About 3 percent of our Moon's | About the same as the Earth's | More than Jupiter's | About twice our Moon's
exercise: What is the largest object in the asteroid belt? | Ceres, about 940 kilometres across | Vesta, about 9,400 kilometres across | Psyche, the size of Mars | Bennu, the size of the Moon
exercise: What is Ceres classed as? | A dwarf planet | A moon of Mars | A comet | A star
exercise: Why is Vesta special for meteorite hunters? | Rock blasted off it by a giant impact still falls to Earth | It is made entirely of gold | It is the closest asteroid to the Earth, so its bits fall here often | It has its own oceans
exercise: What is the asteroid Psyche mostly made of? | Metal | Ice | Gas | Carbon dust
exercise: Which mission returned samples from Bennu in 2023? | OSIRIS-REx | DART | Apollo 17 | New Horizons
exercise: From which asteroids did Japanese probes bring back dust? | Itokawa and Ryugu | Ceres and Vesta | Phobos and Deimos | Bennu and Psyche
exercise: What are near-Earth objects? | Asteroids that cross or come near the Earth's orbit | Satellites and space stations that orbit close to the Earth | Stars that are the very closest ones to us in the night sky | Clouds of space dust that float just above the ground at night
exercise: What did DART do in 2022? | It crashed into Dimorphos and changed its orbit | It landed on Ceres and took photos | It blew up an asteroid that was heading straight for Earth | It brought dust back from Ryugu
exercise: What helped wipe out the dinosaurs about 66 million years ago? | An asteroid about 10 kilometres wide hitting what is now Mexico | A huge flood that covered the whole Earth | Early humans hunting them | A comet hitting the Moon
exercise: Which dinosaurs survived? | The birds | The biggest plant-eaters | The fastest meat-eaters | None at all
exercise: What is a meteor? | The streak of light a space rock makes in the sky | A space rock that has fallen and landed on the ground | A large asteroid in the belt between Mars and Jupiter | A small icy planet with a long, bright tail behind it
exercise: What is a meteorite? | A piece of space rock that reaches the ground | The bright streak of light you see in the sky at night | A small space rock that is still floating out in space | A shooting star that burns up completely in the air
test: In a film, a spaceship swerves through an asteroid belt packed with tumbling rocks. What is the truth? | The belt is mostly empty; asteroids are usually very far apart | Films are right, the rocks are touching | The belt is a solid ring of rock | Ships cannot fly through it at all
test: my bro says the belt is a smashed planet. what do scientists think? | It is leftover material that never joined into a planet | He is right, a whole planet exploded there a long time ago | It is pieces of the Moon | It is ice from Saturn's rings
test: Why did the asteroid belt never form a planet? | Jupiter's strong gravity kept stirring it up | The Sun burnt the rocks before they joined | The asteroids repel each other like magnets | A planet did form there but was later eaten up by Mars
test: You find a heavy black stone that fell from the sky. What should you call it? | A meteorite | A meteor | A meteoroid | A comet
test: Lots of shooting stars tonight! What are they really? | Meteors, small space rocks burning up in our air | Real stars falling right out of the sky and going out | Aeroplanes and satellites with bright flashing lights | Little pieces of the Moon breaking off and falling to us
test: If astronomers spotted an asteroid heading for Earth years ahead, what could be tried? | Hitting it with a spacecraft to nudge its orbit, as DART tested | Waiting for the Moon to catch it | Nothing can ever be done | Shouting a warning so it turns away
test: Why do scientists want samples from asteroids like Bennu? | They are almost unchanged leftovers from the birth of the Solar System | They contain living creatures | They will make the Earth bigger | They are made of melted planets
test: A dinosaur game says all dinosaurs died out. What is the better answer? | Birds survived and are living dinosaurs | All died, birds came from fish | Some big dinosaurs still live in jungles | Crocodiles are the last dinosaurs
test: Grace adds up all the belt's asteroids and expects a planet bigger than the Earth. What would she really get? | Much less than our Moon, only about 3 percent of its mass | Exactly one Earth | A planet bigger than Jupiter | About two Moons
test: Why is Ceres no longer called just an asteroid? | It is big and round enough to be classed as a dwarf planet | It has a ring and two moons | It orbits the Earth | It is made of ice and has a tail
test: ugh another asteroid is gonna kill us all next week says the news. what's the real story? | Astronomers track tens of thousands of near-Earth objects; a scary headline is not the same as a real threat | Yes, one hits every week | Asteroids never come near the Earth | Nobody watches for asteroids
exam: Identify the region in which the greater number of known asteroids are found. | Between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter | Between the orbits of the Earth and Venus | Within the orbit of Mercury | In the vicinity of the Moon
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Double Asteroid Redirection Test of 2022. | A spacecraft collided with Dimorphos and altered its orbit | A spacecraft destroyed an asteroid that was approaching the Earth | A spacecraft returned samples from Bennu | A spacecraft landed upon the dwarf planet Ceres
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a meteorite? | A fragment of extraterrestrial rock recovered upon the ground | The luminous trail produced in the upper atmosphere | A small rocky body orbiting the Sun within the asteroid belt for ever | An icy body possessing an extended tail
exam: Ascertain the aggregate mass of the asteroid belt relative to that of the Moon. | Approximately three per cent thereof | Approximately equal thereto | Approximately thirty times greater | Approximately three hundred times greater
